#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid "Visualize statevectors and density matrices using Qiskit"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Plot quantum states"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<OpenInLabBanner notebookPath=\"guides/plot-quantum-states.ipynb\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"{/*\n"
"  DO NOT EDIT THIS CELL!!!\n"
"  This cell's content is generated automatically by a script. Anything you add\n"
"  here will be removed next time the notebook is run. To add new content, create\n"
"  a new cell before or after this one.\n"
"*/}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Package versions\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The code on this page was developed using the following requirements.\n"
"We recommend using these versions or newer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"In many situations – such as learning or debugging – it's helpful to visualize the state of a quantum computer. Here we assume you already have a particular state from simulation or state tomography.  It's only possible to view the states of small quantum systems.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Using the output from functions\" type=\"tip\">\n"
"All functions on this page return rich objects. When the last line of a code cell outputs these objects, Jupyter notebooks display them below the cell. If you call these functions in some other environments or in scripts, you will need to explicitly show or save the outputs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Most functions return images, which are `matplotlib.Figure` objects. Two options are:\n"
"- Call `.show()` on the returned object to open the image in a new window (assuming your configured matplotlib backend is interactive).\n"
"- Call `.savefig(\"out.png\")` to save the figure to `out.png` in the current working directory. The `savefig()` method takes a path so you can adjust the location and filename where you're saving the output. For example, `plot_state_city(psi).savefig(\"out.png\")`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The LaTeX outputs are `IPython.display.Latex` objects. The best option in a non-Jupyter environment is to avoid this output by either printing the state for a text representation, or switching to the `latex_source` drawer to return a LaTeX source string.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A quantum state is either a density matrix $\\rho$ (Hermitian matrix) or "
"statevector $|\\psi\\rangle$ (complex vector). The density matrix is related"
" to the statevector by\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$$\\rho = |\\psi\\rangle\\langle \\psi|,$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"and is more general, as it can represent mixed states (positive sum of "
"statevectors)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$$\\rho = \\sum_k p_k |\\psi_k\\rangle\\langle \\psi_k |.$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Qiskit represents quantum states through the `Statevector` and "
"`DensityMatrix` classes and provides many visualization functions. See the "
"sections after the following the code cell to see how Qiskit's different "
"visualization functions plot the following quantum state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Tabs>\n"
"<TabItem value=\"LaTeX\" label=\"LaTeX\">\n"
"  While not technically a \"plot\", Qiskit can render LaTeX representations of both `Statevector` and `DensityMatrix` objects that display nicely in Jupyter notebooks. These follow the standard mathematical conventions for writing down quantum states. Read more in [Basics of quantum information: Single systems](/learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/single-systems/introduction).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Statevectors default to \"ket notation\", whereas density matrices are "
"displayed as a 2×2 matrix.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  You can also replace `\"latex\"` with `\"latex_source\"` to get the raw "
"LaTeX string.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</TabItem>\n"
"<TabItem value=\"City\" label=\"City\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  This plot displays the real and imaginary parts of each density matrix "
"element in two three-dimensional bar charts.  It's called a \"city\" plot "
"because the bars resemble skyscrapers in a city. The state we're plotting "
"has the following density matrix.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  $$\n"
"  \\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"  \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2} i}{4}  \\\\\n"
"  \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{1}{4} & 0 & \\frac{i}{4}  \\\\\n"
"  0 & 0 & 0 & 0  \\\\\n"
"  - \\frac{\\sqrt{2} i}{4} & - \\frac{i}{4} & 0 & \\frac{1}{4}  \\\\\n"
"  \\end{bmatrix}\n"
"  =\n"
"  \\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"  \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & 0 & 0 \\\\\n"
"  \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{1}{4} & 0 & 0 \\\\\n"
"  0 & 0 & 0 & 0  \\\\\n"
"  0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{4}  \\\\\n"
"  \\end{bmatrix}\n"
"  + i\n"
"  \\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"  0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2} i}{4}  \\\\\n"
"  0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{i}{4}  \\\\\n"
"  0 & 0 & 0 & 0  \\\\\n"
"  - \\frac{\\sqrt{2} i}{4} & - \\frac{i}{4} & 0 & 0 \\\\\n"
"  \\end{bmatrix}\n"
"  $$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  See the [API "
"documentation](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.visualization.plot_state_city)"
" for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</TabItem>\n"
"<TabItem value=\"Hinton\" label=\"Hinton\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  This plot is very similar to the \"city\" plot, but the magnitude of each "
"element is represented by the size of a square rather than the height of a "
"bar. White squares represent elements with positive values, and black "
"squares represent elements with negative values. The state we're plotting "
"has the following density matrix.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  See the [API "
"documentation](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.visualization.plot_state_hinton)"
" for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</TabItem>\n"
"<TabItem value=\"Pauli vector\" label=\"Pauli vector\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  An observable is a way of measuring a quantum state such that the possible"
" measurement outcomes are real numbers. The expected value of the outcome is"
" also known as the expectation value of the observable on that state, and it"
" can be thought of as the average of infinitely many observations of that "
"state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Tensor products of Pauli matrices are all observables that return +1 or "
"-1. This plot displays the expectation values of the state on different "
"Pauli operators as a bar chart. All density matrices can be written as a sum"
" of these Pauli matrices, weighted by their expectation values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  For example, this state can be written as the sum of terms:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  $$\n"
"  |\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi|\n"
"  =\n"
"  \\tfrac{1}{4}II\n"
"  + \\tfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}IX\n"
"  - \\tfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}XY\n"
"  - \\tfrac{1}{8}YI\n"
"  - \\tfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}YX\n"
"  + \\tfrac{1}{8}YZ\n"
"  + \\tfrac{1}{8}ZI\n"
"  + \\tfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}ZX\n"
"  + \\tfrac{1}{8}ZZ\n"
"  $$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  You can also calculate these coefficients using `SparsePauliOp`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  See the [API "
"documentation](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.visualization.plot_state_paulivec)"
" for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</TabItem>\n"
"<TabItem value=\"Qsphere\" label=\"Qsphere\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  The \"QSphere\" is a Qiskit-unique view of a quantum state in which the "
"amplitude and phase of each element in a statevector is plotted on the "
"surface of a sphere. The thickness of each dot represents the amplitude, and"
" the color represents the phase. For mixed states it will show a sphere for "
"each component.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  See the [API "
"documentation](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.visualization.plot_state_qsphere)"
" for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</TabItem>\n"
"<TabItem value=\"Bloch\" label=\"Bloch\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  The Bloch vector of a qubit state is its expectation value in the X, Y, "
"and Z Pauli observables mapped to the X, Y, and Z axes in three-dimensional "
"space. This plot projects multi-qubit quantum states onto the single-qubit "
"space and plots each qubit on a Bloch sphere. This visualization only shows "
"the expectation values of individual qubits. It can't show correlations "
"between qubits and so can't fully describe entangled quantum states.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  See the [API "
"documentation](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.visualization.plot_bloch_multivector)"
" for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} |00\\rangle+\\frac{1}{2} |01\\rangle- \\frac{i}{2} "
"|11\\rangle$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2} i}{4}  \\\\\n"
" \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{1}{4} & 0 & \\frac{i}{4}  \\\\\n"
" 0 & 0 & 0 & 0  \\\\\n"
" - \\frac{\\sqrt{2} i}{4} & - \\frac{i}{4} & 0 & \\frac{1}{4}  \\\\\n"
" \\end{bmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

